<p>
  I am trapped here again.
</p>

<p>
  This is Baengnyeongdo.
</p>

<p>
  The sea fog has settled in.<br />
  The sea and the sky have already lost their boundary,<br />
  and things disappear one by one, beginning with those farthest away.<br />
  I can count only a few of the dragon’s teeth lined along the beach.<br />
  The rest have entered something white and damp.
</p>

<p>
  Whenever I hear the name <i>dragon’s teeth</i>,<br />
  I imagine the teeth of an actual dragon.<br />
  Something enormous buried beneath the sand,<br />
  with only its teeth left above the surface.
</p>

<p>
  I know what they are made to keep out.<br />
  But when I look at them long enough,<br />
  they begin to seem like the opposite.
</p>

<p>
  As if they are waiting for something<br />
  to come out of the sea.
</p>

<p>
  A 999K and an ARF hang from my back.<br />
  On one side, a bag for spent cartridges pulls my body downward,<br />
  and a lump of metal rests against my arm.
</p>

<p>
  It is heavy.
</p>

<p>
  Strangely, the weight feels familiar, almost welcome.<br />
  Because I already know exactly how heavy it is.
</p>

<p>
  I know which part of my shoulder begins to hurt first,<br />
  how many steps I can take before I have to lift the strap and settle it again,<br />
  and which way to tilt the lump of metal<br />
  so that it hurts my wrist a little less.
</p>

<p>
  Inside my combat boots,<br />
  my big toe is cold.
</p>

<p>
  I don't know if my socks are actually wet.<br />
  The part around my big toe is always cold<br />
  as if it were soaked through.
</p>

<p>
  I shift my weight from side to side.<br />
  My right heel knocks against my left,<br />
  then my left heel knocks against my right.
</p>

<p>
  Tap.<br />
  Tap.
</p>

<p>
  After doing this for a while,<br />
  a small amount of warmth appears inside my boots.
</p>

<p>
  It is never enough to warm the whole foot.<br />
  I wait for the warmth from my heels to travel across the soles<br />
  and reach my big toe.
</p>

<p>
  Most of the time,<br />
  it never does.
</p>

<p>
  As soon as I stop moving,<br />
  the big toe is the first thing to turn cold again.
</p>

<p>
  I don't remember the other toes.
</p>

<p>
  I remember only the big toe.
</p>

<p>
  I remember the password for that day too.
</p>

<p>
  When someone speaks the first half<br />
  from somewhere inside the fog,<br />
  I know the second half.
</p>

<p>
  There is nothing strange about this.
</p>

<p>
  I have been here before.<br />
  I already walk this road.<br />
  I already carry the same weight.<br />
  I already give the same answer.
</p>

<p>
  The problem is that, at the same time,<br />
  it has been a long time since I left this place.
</p>

<p>
  I take the boat away from Baengnyeongdo.<br />
  Years pass.<br />
  I live in different cities and meet people<br />
  whom the person I am now has not yet met.
</p>

<p>
  I remember all of it.
</p>

<p>
  And still,<br />
  the big toe inside my combat boot is cold.
</p>

<p>
  Someone in the fog asks for the password.
</p>

<p>
  I answer.
</p>

<p>
  And I walk again.
</p>

<p>
  This time too,<br />
  I know what is going to happen.
</p>

